{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nints=s['interpretations']\ninv=json.load(open('research.json'))['reselection']['questions'] if False else None\ncur={}\n# find latest interpretation per investigation\nfor i in ints:\n    cur.setdefault(i['investigation_id'],[]).append(i)\nfor k,v in cur.items():\n    last=v[-1]\n    print('='*80)\n    print(k, '| id', last['id'], '| concl', last.get('conclusion'), '| claim', last.get('defect_claim'), '| exp', last.get('experiment_id'))\n    print('sources:', last.get('source_ids'))\n    print('executions:', json.dumps(last.get('executions')))\n    print('COUNTEREVIDENCE:')\n    for c in last.get('counterevidence',[]): print('  -',c)\n    print('LIMITATIONS:')\n    for c in last.get('limitations',[]): print('  -',c)\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"================================================================================\n3b823834c8cb46da98e77d8e988bf5d9-question-0 | id 9630dae6a3094a3c9f82dc76ea57f989-interpretation-0 | concl explained | claim asserted | exp None\nsources: ['verify_leader', 'quorum_size', 'start_stop_repl', 'notify_all', 'verify_vote', 'update_last_appended', 'heartbeat_ack', 'api_verify_leader', 'lease_check', 'config_validation', 'reload_config', 'elect_vote_collection', 'prevote_vote_collection', 'vote_grant_side', 'follower_election_trigger', 'follower_campaign_logic', 'lease_check_granularity', 'random_timeout', 'vote_leader_known_check', 'prevote_leader_known_check', 'follower_clears_leader', 'api_apply_future', 'fsm_apply_values', 'api_verify_doc']\nexecutions: {\"b02d5ff23a504a8ea38116085ba8e23c\": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], \"107a6d6c816d448da2b7ff6e3145bac1\": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], \"01617c5662f34c3eb21857602f6cac4f\": [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11], \"2f645950e0334342b1f7392ed1c1f286\": [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11], \"ca148c05672f478186684b8c168d9601\": [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The leadership lease still bounds the ordinary case: without a stalled main loop the lease case is served no later than LeaderLeaseTimeout, which is why the six-trial probe needed a 900ms store stall to reach the condition.\n  - The witness depends on a non-voter in the configuration and reachable from the stalled leader; the earlier controls show that without any acknowledgement the same call fails (no-peer control) and that a voter acknowledgement is the legitimate path.\n  - The stalled leader never adopted the successor's term (leader_term_after stayed 2 in every trial), which is what makes the two leaders coexist and the verdict stale.\n  - The split between success and leadership loss varies between executions of the same probe: the first run returned success in three of six trials and leadership loss in the other three, while the repeat returned success in five of six (only the 250ms reconnection answered leadership loss). Across both runs eight of twelve trials returned success, and the successor overlap held in all twelve, so the overlap is what reproduces while the verdict remains a per-trial choice.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - The end-to-end read is demonstrated in two of five trials of the latest probe: the stale read needs the verify to return success, and the other three trials answered leadership loss, in which case a correct caller would not go on to read the FSM at all.\n  - The witness rests on two executions of the same fixed probe (receipts 01617c5662f34c3eb21857602f6cac4f and 2f645950e0334342b1f7392ed1c1f286), twelve trials in total, of which eight returned success; the probe is not repeated under different timeouts or stall lengths.\n  - The verdict arrives when the stall releases the main loop (931-932ms in every trial), so the success is returned at the first loop iteration after the acknowledgement had already been counted, not while the loop was blocked.\n  - In-memory transports in one process with 200ms timeouts and a 900ms stall; multi-host clocks and process suspensions are not covered.\n  - The stall is a wrapper around the log store that blocks StoreLogs on the leader's main thread, and the non-voter is reconnected by transport calls; both are supplied conditions rather than faults observed in a deployment.\n================================================================================\n627b3ef0c6e049bfb836e40ffbdce875-question-1 | id c0b578f29c9a4e6a8b6d3704ee554146-interpretation-0 | concl explained | claim asserted | exp None\nsources: ['api_transfer_to_server', 'transfer_target_lookup', 'timeout_now', 'candidate_skips_prevote', 'vote_term_bump', 'transfer_lost_leadership', 'pick_server_voters', 'follower_vote_grant', 'docs_transfer', 'start_stop_repl']\nexecutions: {\"b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd\": [1, 3], \"23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0\": [0, 1, 2, 3]}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The API comment does not state that the target must be a voter, and a caller who names only voters never reaches this path, so the defect depends on an accepted calling pattern rather than on the library's own choice of target.\n  - LeadershipTransfer() without an explicit target filters non-voters in pickServer, which shows the intended transfer target set is the voter set and supports reading the targeted call as requiring the same premise.\n  - A TimeoutNow that cannot be delivered (unreachable target) is reported as an error by the transfer path, so not every unusable target is silently accepted.\n  - The interruption is bounded: the remaining voters re-elect after their election timers, and the control case shows the probe does observe a completed transfer, so the outcome is a wrong success report and a leadership interruption rather than a cluster that never recovers.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - The reported-result and interval observations rest on two executions of this probe (receipts b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd and 23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0); across them the leader holding office at the end differed (in the first, the previous leader was Leader again at term 4; in the second, another voter), so the identity of the successor is timing dependent and is recorded rather than asserted.\n  - The leaderless interval is an observed span at 1ms sampling resolution between a 200ms election timeout and a single-process in-memory transport; it is not a bound for configurations with different timeouts, and it does not distinguish time spent campaigning from time waiting for election timers.\n  - The control case with a voter target finished in about one millisecond, so its sampling window is too short for its zero leaderless count to say anything about longer gaps.\n  - The claim concerns the reported result of the call, the requested target's inability to win and the observed interruption; it does not assert data loss or that the cluster fails to recover, since a voter is elected afterwards.\n  - The probe uses one process with real Raft instances and in-memory transports, so it exercises the leader's own transfer, election and vote paths but not process-level partitions or multi-host clocks.\n================================================================================\n1f661a68c21d426189d35f4a76d491f4-question-0 | id 983e6d23976b4e4e9eff6a3987874db1-interpretation-0 | concl violated | claim refuted | exp 1f661a68c21d426189d35f4a76d491f4-experiment\nsources: ['verify_leader', 'quorum_size', 'lease_check', 'lease_check_granularity', 'start_stop_repl', 'notify_all', 'update_last_appended', 'verify_vote']\nexecutions: {\"2e5f37f634834b8197227626ed6d99b7\": [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15]}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The stall model, not the source, is what the trials refute: a slower or differently placed stall could change which case the main loop serves first, and a networked deployment makes each RPC round trip orders of magnitude slower than the loop iteration, which is the opposite direction from the one that produced these endpoints.\n  - In two trials the failure was ErrNotLeader rather than ErrLeadershipLost, showing the queued request sometimes never reached verifyLeader at all; those trials measure the queue being drained after leadership loss rather than the race between the acknowledgement and the step-down.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - One execution of eight trials is cited; the endpoints were uniform (0/8 success) but the reported interleavings are inferred from the two error values, not sampled directly.\n  - The stall is a wrapper around the log store that blocks the leader's main loop inside StoreLogs; the source comment on dispatchLogs shows the store write happens on the main thread, so the model matches that path, but a stall elsewhere (for example inside the lease check itself or a host suspension) is not covered.\n  - HeartbeatTimeout = ElectionTimeout = LeaderLeaseTimeout = 200ms, stall 600ms, in-process in-memory transports, three voters plus one non-voter with only the non-voter reachable from the stalled leader.\n================================================================================\nb2ccc787c9384b008b5f9c668746f760-question-0 | id d33dfeeee4124704bae38664b5b63da2-interpretation-0 | concl explained | claim asserted | exp 4916079656954a2b81e3a6bfdb12f816-experiment\nsources: ['api_verify_future', 'api_barrier_future', 'api_apply_future', 'defer_error_block', 'verify_channel_buffer', 'shutdown_close', 'apply_channel_unbuffered', 'batch_apply_ch_config', 'test_todo_stuck']\nexecutions: {\"b0748389f0ab44d89142c25404fe247c\": [1, 7, 13, 25, 27, 29, 31, 33, 35, 3, 9, 15, 5, 11, 17, 19], \"be69cdfff5274bc391795508e987429d\": [1, 7, 13, 25, 27, 29, 3, 9, 15, 5, 11, 17, 19]}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The racing form did not reproduce in the later revisions (0 of 128 calls per case in two runs), so the earlier 9 and 41 are a timing-dependent manifestation rather than a rate.\n  - Without BatchApplyCh the apply path is never affected: Barrier and Apply answered every post-shutdown call in all three runs (indices 3, 9, 15 and 5, 11, 17 here), so the defect reaches the apply path only under a configuration whose own comment already warns that it breaks the Apply timeout guarantee.\n  - Roughly half of the calls in each affected case are answered with the documented shutdown error rather than stranded, so the outcome is a per-call select choice, and no call that was answered carried a wrong outcome.\n  - The repository's test file records the same class as a known TODO, which is evidence about intent, not a reason to treat an unbounded block as acceptable.\n  - Over-subscribing verifyCh five-fold did not remove the strand: the 320-call cases stranded 2, 3 and 2 of eight post-shutdown calls, against 3, 7 and 5 of eight for the 128-call cases, so the outcome is not decided by spare capacity being available when the loop exits.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - The post-shutdown counts per case come from three trials of eight calls each (VerifyLeader 11 of 24 stranded; batched Barrier 11 of 24; batched Apply 13 of 24), so they bound the mechanism rather than a population rate.\n  - The strand is classified by a 500ms window; permanence is inferred from the source, since no reader of the affected channel remains and these futures carry no shutdown channel.\n  - Single-voter node, in-memory transport, 50ms timeouts, one process: no peer round trip or networked shutdown is involved.\n  - The over-subscribed cases carry 320 concurrent racing calls, five times the 64 verifyCh slots, so a large share of sends could not be accepted; the buffer still ended with free slots and the strand persisted, which means occupancy at the exit is not what the probe controls.\n================================================================================\n0bf310835ed043eca09a1668b327ab7b-question-0 | id 9add019f72dc45228e969af0be11745f-interpretation-0 | concl explained | claim refuted | exp None\nsources: ['process_config_committed', 'truncation_reset_latest', 'append_commit_update', 'config_change_if_stable', 'leader_commit_handling']\nexecutions: {}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The negative result rests on the leader gate rather than on anything the follower verifies: a leader that wrote configuration entries outside that gate, or a log that already contained two uncommitted configurations, would make the shortcut unsound.\n  - The appendEntries commit handling only advances the follower's committed configuration when the leader's commit index has reached its latest configuration index, so the follower's committed field can lag or lead its own commit index depending on which path set it last.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - The account is source-based: no execution traced a follower through an append of a configuration entry, a withheld commit and a forced truncation, so the reasoning about index ordering is not measured.\n  - The argument about identical entries below the conflict index relies on Raft's log-matching property rather than on an observation in this snapshot.\n  - It covers the library's own configuration API and does not consider a user writing LogConfiguration entries into the log directly.\n================================================================================\n1995fd2023c948c99b650dce1b597cd6-question-0 | id dc74503bfa27432892dddd69e274de4f-interpretation-0 | concl violated | claim asserted | exp 60915351a519403a9c3ee2b96fae7ce2-experiment\nsources: ['batching_fsm_iface', 'config_store_iface', 'fsm_single_apply', 'fsm_batch_apply', 'next_configuration_commands', 'fsm_batch_delegation']\nexecutions: {\"61281db3f55e4b03879ff0ae87c15000\": [1, 3], \"a5809ed4b5024b17adfebf093d3316f5\": [1, 3], \"b2eeee31959146808c2a56f9a67b14f4\": [1, 3, 5, 7]}\nCOUNTEREVIDENCE:\n  - The configuration entry is not lost: BatchingFSM documents that ApplyBatch receives LogConfiguration entries, and the receipt shows all 6 deliveries there, so an application handling both paths is unaffected.\n  - The combination is unusual rather than forbidden: nothing in either interface documents mutual exclusion, but an application that implements only one of them sees the documented behaviour, as the control case shows.\n  - The measured commands do not exhaust the API: DemoteVoter and the deprecated Promote command were not exercised, although they produce the same LogConfiguration entry type and take the same apply path.\nLIMITATIONS:\n  - Interactions are measured in three executions of the same lineage, all agreeing (dual-interface FSM 0 StoreConfiguration calls with 5 or 6 ApplyBatch configuration entries; control 6 and 0), but all use three-voter clusters built by the repository's own helper with a recorder shared across the cluster's nodes.\n  - The consequence for an application's recovery is argued from the interface's stated purpose rather than demonstrated by recovering an FSM that missed the callback.\n  - DemoteVoter and the deprecated Promote command were not measured; the three commands that were cover a voter address change, an addition and a removal.\n  - The three measured commands are a voter address change, an addition and a removal; that DemoteVoter and the deprecated Promote command take the same apply path is read from the source, where applyBatch is the only entry point the FSM loop uses and applySingle is the only caller of StoreConfiguration.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id']=='9add019f72dc45228e969af0be11745f-interpretation-0':\n        print(i['rationale'])\n        print('---- sources ----')\n        for sid in i['source_ids']: print(sid)\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id']=='9add019f72dc45228e969af0be11745f-interpretation-0':\n        print(i['rationale'])\n        print('---- sources ----')\n        for sid in i['source_ids']: print(sid)\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"The question was whether a follower can hold a committed configuration that was never committed and then truncate its log so that its latest configuration names a server with no entry behind it. The trace answers no for a leader that uses this library's configuration API, and the reason is the leader's own gate rather than the follower's care: configurationChangeChIfStable returns the change channel only while latestIndex equals committedIndex and the leader's commit index has reached the first entry of its term, so the library never lets a leader append a second configuration entry while the first is uncommitted. That makes processConfigurationLogEntry's shortcut - advancing the committed configuration to the previous latest one before installing the new entry - sound, because the previous latest configuration is precisely the one that must have committed for the new entry to exist. The follower's state can therefore read as committed while the follower's own commit index lags, but it does not name a configuration the cluster never committed. The truncation half is closed by geometry rather than by the shortcut: the fallback sets the latest configuration to the committed one at index I, and the truncation starts at the first conflicting index X where the follower's entry differs in term from the leader's. Entries below X are identical to the leader's, and a configuration that was genuinely committed must be present in the log of whichever server won the election afterwards, so X cannot be at or below I and the entry at I survives the DeleteRange. What would break this is a leader that appends two uncommitted configuration entries, which the gate above prevents in this implementation; I record that as the assumption the negative result rests on rather than as a second finding.\n---- sources ----\nprocess_config_committed\ntruncation_reset_latest\nappend_commit_update\nconfig_change_if_stable\nleader_commit_handling\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
